{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n67 \n \n \n \nWeek 7: Nov. 26 \u2013 Dec. 2 \nOn GMT 328, we ignited nine inverse flames for ACME Flame Design. For these tests, an oxygen/nitrogen \nmixture flowed from the burner into a combustion chamber, which was filled with an ethylene/nitrogen \nmixture. For one of these tests, we observed our longest sustained flame, which burned for over 14 \nminutes. At that time, operational constraints required its termination. For comparison, flame durations \nfor all ACME experiments are typically shorter than two minutes with the longest\u2014until now\u2014under six \nminutes. This 14+ minute flame has led to new understanding of the stability of spherical flames. \n \nWhile test measurements did show that this long-duration flame was slowly weakening with time, it might \nhave burned significantly longer. We have found with higher burner flow rates that flames grow with time \nand extinguish due to radiative heat loss. If the flow rate is too low, the flame\u2019s small size leads to excessive \nburner heating. But under the right conditions, the flame can burn for minutes and perhaps even tens of \nminutes. \n \n \nFigure 1. Flame Design Long-Duration Inverse Flame \n \nThis false-colored image was taken about halfway through the 14+ minute test. The blue in the image has \nbeen enhanced so that the dim flame is visible in addition to the ceramic fiber, which is glowing at the top \nof the flame. We use the fibers for optical determination of the flame\u2019s temperature. \n \nWeek 9: Dec. 10-16 \nOn GMT 337, Petr Dubrov and Mark Vande Hei replaced the gas bottle at CIR Manifold #4 to support \ncontinuation of Flame Design test points. On GMT 340 and 349, we ignited 28 inverse flames for ACME \nFlame Design. For these tests, we filled a combustion chamber with a mixture of nitrogen and gaseous \nfuel (ethylene) and then flowed a mixture of nitrogen and oxygen from the burner. For both test days, the \nprimary test parameters included varying the oxygen concentration and flow rate from the burner.", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 67}}